{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"DOI:10.7554/elife.03896","resultType":"core","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":25,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"25402831","source":"MED","pmid":"25402831","pmcid":"PMC4270066","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC4270066"]},"doi":"10.7554/elife.03896","title":"Reinstatement of long-term memory following erasure of its behavioral and synaptic expression in Aplysia.","authorString":"Chen S, Cai D, Pearce K, Sun PY, Roberts AC, Glanzman DL.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Chen S","lastName":"Chen","firstName":"Shanping","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Integrative Biology and Physiology, University of California, Los Angeles, Los Angeles, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Cai D","lastName":"Cai","firstName":"Diancai","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Integrative Biology and Physiology, University of California, Los Angeles, Los Angeles, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Pearce K","lastName":"Pearce","firstName":"Kaycey","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Integrative Biology and Physiology, University of California, Los Angeles, Los Angeles, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Sun PY","lastName":"Sun","firstName":"Philip Y-W","initials":"PY","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4428-6031"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Integrative Biology and Physiology, University of California, Los Angeles, Los Angeles, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Roberts AC","lastName":"Roberts","firstName":"Adam C","initials":"AC","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8596-8552"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Integrative Biology and Physiology, University of California, Los Angeles, Los Angeles, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Glanzman DL","lastName":"Glanzman","firstName":"David L","initials":"DL","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Integrative Biology and Physiology, University of California, Los Angeles, Los Angeles, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8596-8552"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4428-6031"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","plain_english","recommended_reviews","wikipedia","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"3","journalIssueId":2216331,"dateOfPublication":"2014 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2014,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Elife","title":"eLife","essn":"2050-084X","issn":"2050-084X","isoabbreviation":"Elife","nlmid":"101579614"},"printPublicationDate":"2014-11-01"},"pubYear":"2014","pageInfo":"e03896","abstractText":"Long-term memory (LTM) is believed to be stored in the brain as changes in synaptic connections. Here, we show that LTM storage and synaptic change can be dissociated. Cocultures of Aplysia sensory and motor neurons were trained with spaced pulses of serotonin, which induces long-term facilitation. Serotonin (5HT) triggered growth of new presynaptic varicosities, a synaptic mechanism of long-term sensitization. Following 5HT training, two antimnemonic treatments-reconsolidation blockade and inhibition of PKM--caused the number of presynaptic varicosities to revert to the original, pretraining value. Surprisingly, the final synaptic structure was not achieved by targeted retraction of the 5HT-induced varicosities but, rather, by an apparently arbitrary retraction of both 5HT-induced and original synapses. In addition, we find evidence that the LTM for sensitization persists covertly after its apparent elimination by the same antimnemonic treatments that erase learning-related synaptic growth. These results challenge the idea that stable synapses store long-term memories.","affiliation":"Department of Integrative Biology and Physiology, University of California, Los Angeles, Los Angeles, United States.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Research Support, U.S. Gov't, Non-P.H.S.","Journal Article","Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"National Institute of Mental Health","grantId":"R01 MH096120","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIMH NIH HHS","acronym":"MH","grantId":"R01 MH096120","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Division of Integrative Organismal Systems","grantId":"IOS 1121690","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke","grantId":"R01 NS029563","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NINDS NIH HHS","acronym":"NS","grantId":"R01 NS029563","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Synapses","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DE","qualifierName":"drug effects"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Aplysia","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DE","qualifierName":"drug effects"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EN","qualifierName":"enzymology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Serotonin","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PD","qualifierName":"pharmacology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Histone Deacetylases","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Protein Kinase C","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"AI","qualifierName":"antagonists & inhibitors"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"met